 pairs a durable Republican incumbent, David Valadao, against Democrat Randy Villegas in a seat that leans Democratic by only about a point. Valadao has repeatedly won here even when the district's fundamentals pointed the other way, and he holds a large cash-on-hand advantage heading into the fall. Villegas has raised solidly and drawn heavy outside spending from both parties, while Democratic groups have released polls showing him ahead.\n\nThe broader environment favors Democrats, and most expert raters now call the seat a likely or lean Democratic flip, with prediction markets pricing Villegas as the clear favorite. Still, with no independent general-election polling available and Valadao's incumbency history, uncertainty remains high, and the race sits as a Democratic lean rather than anything stronger.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7982, "margin": 5.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8298, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8404, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8532, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8134, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7945, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8475, "margin": 6.5}]}